# `trace` names the debugging intrinsic, so nothing else may be called that. A
# declaration of the name would not shadow it — a `trace(..)` call is rewritten
# before any name is resolved, so the declaration would simply never be
# reached — which is worth a diagnostic rather than silence.

fn trace(x: i64) -> i64 { x }

fn main() {
    trace(1);
}
--- errors ---
error: `trace` is reserved for a compiler intrinsic (`trace(expr)` prints the expression and its value, and yields it), so a function may not be called that: the call is rewritten before any name could shadow it, so the declaration would never be reached
